(4335118, 622510, 52549, '<td valign="top">Superb, serene image. Big congratulation on the yellow, Sam.</td>', 'Superb, serene image. Big congratulation on the yellow, Sam.', '2007-12-26 01:23:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335138, 623775, 60165, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by B74A:</b></div><hr/><i> Ribbon potential. Don''t get restless nights, but good luck! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I see you did not <i>need</i> good luck, William - way to go! Northern Light and Ocean Shores usually go down well in challenges, and this is no exception. Stunning picture, congratulations with your blue ribbon for a Christmas present ...</td>', 'Originally posted by B74A: Ribbon potential. Don''t get restless nights, but good luck!   I see you did not need good luck, William - way to go! Northern Light and Ocean Shores usually go down well in challenges, and this is no exception. Stunning picture, congratulations with your blue ribbon for a Christmas present ...', '2007-12-26 01:35:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335145, 623680, 60165, '<td valign="top">With more 9s and 10s than 1s, 2s and 3s together, I am content at hitting close to 6.1 with this shot.<br/> <br/>I am holding my camera on the handrail of a travellator or moving sidewalk (I don''t know what the proper name is), while passing two christmas trees. The black stripe in the lower left corner is the handrail, and all the lights are obviously ... lights from the christmas trees. Looking at the dots of the light makes me wonder whether this is the vibration of the handrail drive, into the camera, or whether long exposure pictures are actually a mix of a series of exposures? I have seen this before, and had it been with a film camera, I would not have been in doubt it was due to the vibration.<br/> <br/>Balloon was picked up from the floor, as an airline obviously had been giving out balloons to their younger passengers.<br/> <br/>Thank you for your scores, especially thank you to the person who gave me the 1 score - nice Christmas present!</td>', 'With more 9s and 10s than 1s, 2s and 3s together, I am content at hitting close to 6.1 with this shot. I am holding my camera on the handrail of a travellator or moving sidewalk (I don''t know what the proper name is), while passing two christmas trees. The black stripe in the lower left corner is the handrail, and all the lights are obviously ... lights from the christmas trees. Looking at the dots of the light makes me wonder whether this is the vibration of the handrail drive, into the camera, or whether long exposure pictures are actually a mix of a series of exposures? I have seen this before, and had it been with a film camera, I would not have been in doubt it was due to the vibration. Balloon was picked up from the floor, as an airline obviously had been giving out balloons to their younger passengers. Thank you for your scores, especially thank you to the person who gave me the 1 score - nice Christmas present!', '2007-12-26 01:45:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335147, 622510, 31889, '<td valign="top">Gratz Sam on your second ribbon and qualification for the next Masters Challenge, (whenever it comes). :)</td>', 'Gratz Sam on your second ribbon and qualification for the next Masters Challenge, (whenever it comes). :)', '2007-12-26 01:46:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335181, 623576, 60165, '<td valign="top"><br/>Congrats, Mark, I liked this one a lot - and you did better than me :-)<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by fastforward:</b></div><hr/><i> I can''t see how this conforms to basic editing with the selective colour changes you''ve made... </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The reds look a little boosted, but if you look around, you will see red tints all over, so I think it is OK. I would expect it to be asked for validation, though, so Mark, you might as well upload the original (resized) here for the rest of us to see ...<br/> <br/>Happy New Year!</td>', 'Congrats, Mark, I liked this one a lot - and you did better than me :-) Originally posted by fastforward: I can''t see how this conforms to basic editing with the selective colour changes you''ve made...   The reds look a little boosted, but if you look around, you will see red tints all over, so I think it is OK. I would expect it to be asked for validation, though, so Mark, you might as well upload the original (resized) here for the rest of us to see ... Happy New Year!', '2007-12-26 02:28:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335185, 623685, 60165, '<td valign="top"><br/>Congrats on your score ... flat? I like it this way, though a night shot would probably do it well, too!<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by B74A:</b></div><hr/><i> Nice! First impression: London, but does not look like that. Second impression: Kuala Lumpur - but where are the other buildings, as well as the path between the two towers? So ... I look forward to hear where this is from! 7 </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>So it <i>was</i> KL ... I see now the Petronas tower <i>escape route connection bridge</i> is just down in the trees. What is this place called in KL, as I want to go there some day ...</td>', 'Congrats on your score ... flat? I like it this way, though a night shot would probably do it well, too! Originally posted by B74A: Nice! First impression: London, but does not look like that. Second impression: Kuala Lumpur - but where are the other buildings, as well as the path between the two towers? So ... I look forward to hear where this is from! 7   So it was KL ... I see now the Petronas tower escape route connection bridge is just down in the trees. What is this place called in KL, as I want to go there some day ...', '2007-12-26 02:31:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335188, 623514, 60165, '<td valign="top"><br/>Congrats on your personal high! Seems like long exposure train shots can compete with Northern Light and Seashores!<br/> <br/>Allow me to show you what kept a personal high for me for a while, from the electricity challenge:<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=398291" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/558/120/398291.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/558/120/398291.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/558/120/398291.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>But in a few days, it will move down to second place, as I am looking at a 6.6+ score in Perspective :-)<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by B74A:</b></div><hr/><i> One of my best pictures so far is a bit similar to this (electricity challenge). This is executed really well - the empty platform and the clean stripes of the train makes a smooth but dramatic effect. Meets challenge and well done! </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Congrats on your personal high! Seems like long exposure train shots can compete with Northern Light and Seashores! Allow me to show you what kept a personal high for me for a while, from the electricity challenge:   But in a few days, it will move down to second place, as I am looking at a 6.6+ score in Perspective :-) Originally posted by B74A: One of my best pictures so far is a bit similar to this (electricity challenge). This is executed really well - the empty platform and the clean stripes of the train makes a smooth but dramatic effect. Meets challenge and well done! ', '2007-12-26 02:37:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(4335192, 623042, 60165, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by B74A:</b></div><hr/><i> Interesting! A bit like mine, but better, haha! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Right, you rank about twice as high as me - merry Christmas!<br/> <br/>Congrats on your score :-)</td>', 'Originally posted by B74A: Interesting! A bit like mine, but better, haha!   Right, you rank about twice as high as me - merry Christmas! Congrats on your score :-)', '2007-12-26 02:41:57.000000', NULL, 0),
